{"type":"result","result":"Now I have enough information. Let me compile the review findings:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Broad exception handler swallows all exceptions in install_templates\",\n      \"category\": \"error-handling\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/install_templates.py\",\n      \"line\": 78,\n      \"line_range_end\": 79,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": \"except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 — surface any IO failure\\n    failed.append(f\\\"{rel}: {exc}\\\")\",\n      \"why\": \"The BLE001 noqa suppresses ruff's 'blind except' warning, but the comment justification is weak. While the exception is logged, catching all Exception types can mask programming errors (AttributeError, TypeError, KeyError) that should crash loudly rather than being silently logged as 'installation failures'. The intent is to catch IO errors (PermissionError, OSError), but the handler is too broad.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Narrow the exception handler to specific expected IO failures: `except (PermissionError, OSError, IOError, FileNotFoundError) as exc:` and remove the noqa. If truly unexpected errors occur, let them propagate to the caller so the installer fails fast rather than continuing with incomplete state.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"src/superclaude/cli/install_agents.py:58\", \"src/superclaude/cli/install_commands.py:59\", \"src/superclaude/cli/install_core.py:55\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Inconsistent 'failed' handling guard across installer modules\",\n      \"category\": \"correctness\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/install_skills.py\",\n      \"line\": 114,\n      \"line_range_end\": 115,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"if not installed and not skipped and not served_by_command:\\n    return True, \\\"No skills to install\\\"\",\n      \"why\": \"install_skills.py is the only installer that omits `and not failed` from the empty-source guard, yet it returns `(True, message)` instead of `(False, message)` like the other installers. This is inconsistent with the pattern established in install_templates.py, install_agents.py, install_commands.py, and install_core.py (all fixed in this PR to include `and not failed`). The success=True on an empty result is also semantically questionable.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Add `and not failed` to the guard at line 114 to match the pattern in other installers, and consider whether success=True is the correct return for 'nothing to do'. The other four installers return False for the empty-source sentinel, which is more consistent with the install failure surface.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"src/superclaude/cli/install_templates.py:97\", \"src/superclaude/cli/install_agents.py:80\", \"src/superclaude/cli/install_commands.py:79\", \"src/superclaude/cli/install_core.py:80\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Missing test coverage for install_templates.py\",\n      \"category\": \"tests\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"tests/cli/test_install_failures.py\",\n      \"line\": 1,\n      \"line_range_end\": null,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": null,\n      \"why\": \"test_install_failures.py adds parametrized regression tests for the 'not failed' guard fix across four installers (templates, core, agents, commands), but there are no other tests for install_templates.py's core functionality (source discovery, subdirectory preservation, protected-path rejection). The other installer modules (install_agents, install_commands, install_core) likely have similar coverage gaps.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Add unit tests for install_templates.py covering: (1) _get_templates_source() resolution (editable vs wheel layout), (2) subdirectory structure preservation (workflow/ and documents/), (3) _PROTECTED_TARGET_SUBDIRS rejection (agent-memory/ must never be written), (4) list_available_templates() and list_installed_templates() accuracy. Follow the pattern in tests/cli/prd/test_path_resolution.py for path isolation via monkeypatch.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"tests/cli/prd/test_path_resolution.py:1\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Potential Path.relative_to() ValueError in list_available_templates\",\n      \"category\": \"correctness\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/install_templates.py\",\n      \"line\": 136,\n      \"line_range_end\": 142,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": \"return sorted(\\n    str(f.relative_to(source))\\n    for f in source.rglob(\\\"*\\\")\\n    if f.is_file()\\n    and \\\"__pycache__\\\" not in f.parts\\n    and not any(p in _PROTECTED_TARGET_SUBDIRS for p in f.relative_to(source).parts)\\n)\",\n      \"why\": \"The comprehension calls `f.relative_to(source)` twice per file: once for the protected-path check, again for stringification. If the rglob() somehow returns a symlink that escapes the source tree (e.g., a symlink loop or external target), relative_to() will raise ValueError. While unlikely in controlled template directories, the code has no guard against this case.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Bind the relative path once at the start of the comprehension to avoid double computation and eliminate the ValueError surface: `rel = f.relative_to(source) for f in ... if f.is_file() and ... and not any(p in _PROTECTED_TARGET_SUBDIRS for p in rel.parts)`. This also applies to list_installed_templates() at line 150.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"src/superclaude/cli/install_templates.py:150\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"low\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"install_templates not registered in pyproject.toml entry points\",\n      \"category\": \"architecture\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/main.py\",\n      \"line\": 77,\n      \"line_range_end\": 81,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": \"from .install_templates import (\\n    install_templates,\\n    list_available_templates,\\n    list_installed_templates,\\n)\",\n      \"why\": \"install_templates is imported and invoked in main.py's install command, but the diff shows no corresponding pyproject.toml entry point for a standalone `superclaude templates` subcommand (similar to how `superclaude mcp` or `superclaude doctor` exist). This is inconsistent with the modular CLI design where each installer typically has a dedicated subcommand.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"If the project intends to expose template installation separately (e.g., `superclaude templates install`, `superclaude templates list`), add a CLI entry point similar to install_skill.py or install_skills.py. If not, document why templates are only accessible via `superclaude install` and not as a standalone subcommand.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"src/superclaude/cli/install_agents.py:1\", \"src/superclaude/cli/install_commands.py:1\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"cross_cutting_observations\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Inconsistent exception handling patterns across installer modules\",\n      \"category\": \"anti-pattern\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"affected_files\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/install_templates.py\",\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/install_agents.py\",\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/install_commands.py\",\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/install_core.py\"\n      ],\n      \"why\": \"All four installers use broad `except Exception` handlers around shutil.copy2() (lines 58-59 in install_agents, 56-59 in install_commands, 52-55 in install_core, 74-79 in install_templates). Three of them have no justification comment; install_templates has a BLE001 noqa with a weak rationale. This anti-pattern masks programming errors and makes debugging harder. A more disciplined approach would catch only expected IO failures (PermissionError, OSError, FileNotFoundError) and let unexpected errors propagate.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Standardize exception handling across all four installers: replace `except Exception` with `except (PermissionError, OSError, IOError, FileNotFoundError)`. Add a comment explaining why each exception type is expected (e.g., PermissionError when target is read-only, OSError for disk-full conditions). Remove BLE001 noqa suppressions.\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Duplication in path resolution logic across installer modules\",\n      \"category\": \"architecture\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"affected_files\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/install_templates.py\",\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/install_agents.py\",\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/install_commands.py\",\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/install_core.py\"\n      ],\n      \"why\": \"Each installer module has its own `_get_<thing>_source()` function with slightly different resolution logic. install_templates._get_templates_source() checks editable then wheel layout; install_agents._get_agents_source() (line 107+) and install_commands._get_commands_source() use variations of the same pattern. The subtle differences (e.g., install_templates uses `package_root / '_src' / 'superclaude' / ...` while others may not) create a maintenance burden and increase the risk of layout-discovery bugs when the packaging changes.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Extract a shared `_resolve_package_resource(relative_path: Path, resource_name: str) -> Path` helper into a new `src/superclaude/cli/_paths.py` module. Each installer calls it with its specific relative path (e.g., 'templates', 'agents', 'commands/sc', 'core'). This centralizes the editable-vs-wheel layout logic and reduces duplication.\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"SoT layering between .claude/ and src/superclaude/templates/ properly enforced\",\n      \"category\": \"architecture\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"affected_files\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/templates/workflow/05_prd_template.md\",\n        \".claude/templates/workflow/05_prd_template.md\",\n        \"Makefile\"\n      ],\n      \"why\": \"The diff correctly implements the SoT rule documented in CLAUDE.md: src/superclaude/templates/ is the canonical source, .claude/templates/ is the dev copy synced via `make sync-dev`, and `make verify-sync` enforces drift detection. The Makefile additions (lines 1758-1767, 1784-1811) correctly exclude *.legacy-rf-project.md from the reverse check (since .claude/ can have project-specific overrides not meant for distribution). This layering is consistent with the skills/ and agents/ sync pattern.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"No action needed. The implementation correctly follows the established SoT pattern. Consider adding a comment in the Makefile explaining why *.legacy-rf-project.md is excluded from the reverse sync check (to document the intentional asymmetry for future maintainers).\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"PRD path resolution correctly migrated to .claude/-anchored lookups\",\n      \"category\": \"architecture\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"affected_files\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/prd/models.py\",\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/prd/config.py\",\n        \"tests/cli/prd/test_path_resolution.py\"\n      ],\n      \"why\": \"The PR fixes the FileNotFoundError regression by anchoring PRD skill refs and template lookups to .claude/ (project-local first, then user-home) instead of the old relative-path pattern that silently resolved against CWD. The _default_skill_refs_dir() and _default_template_path() helpers in models.py (lines 38-83) correctly implement a three-candidate fallback (cwd/.claude, home/.claude, dev-tree) and always return an absolute path (even on tota